you for having received food from an angel; for demons, too, can bring bread, even steal it and offer it. But this bread does not nourish the body, for the bread of demons stinks, and if bread is from the devil, the soul is disturbed when it sees it. But if the bread is from an angel, the soul is not disturbed but remains in peace and tranquility. At that moment when someone is worthy to receive bread from the hands of angels, reason first provides discernment, if we recall the words of the Apostle, saying: ‘The food of the mature is for those who, because of their perfection, have trained their senses to discern what is good and what is evil.’	Bohairic Lausiac History - Part 3	urn:cts:copticLit:lausiac.boh_lausiac.amelineau_ed:38	0	bohairic
